From 8d0c5dffed7394c0a60d01ec678ded735580c6fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romeu Melur <romeumello@icloud.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:20:05 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] virtio/net/unixgram: optionally keep the virtio-net header on
 the socket

The unixgram backend strips the virtio-net header from every frame the
guest sends and writes a zeroed one for every frame it receives, so a
userspace network proxy behind it can't use segmentation or checksum
offload: a guest that negotiates VIRTIO_NET_F_HOST_TSO4 would hand over
64 KiB TCP segments the proxy has no way to recognize.

Add NetFlags::VNET_HDR (KRUN_NET_FLAGS_VNET_HDR). With it, both
new_unixgram_fd and new_unixgram_path keep the 12-byte header in front
of each datagram, in both directions, and the path variant sizes the
send buffer for the header. Without it nothing changes.

On macOS, with a gvisor-based proxy that strips the header itself and a
guest with MTU 1500 and HOST_TSO4/6, guest-to-host TCP went from 1.6-1.9
to 7.4-8.9 Gbit/s.

Generated-by: Claude Code: claude-opus-5.5
Signed-off-by: Romeu Melur <romeumello@icloud.com>
